  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Show that one cannot construct a triangle $\\triangle ABC$ for which $\\angle A=45^{\\circ}$, $a=2\\text{ cm}$ and $c=4\\text{ cm}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No such triangle exists, since the sine rule gives $\\sin C=\\sqrt{2}>1$.",
      "worked": "If such a triangle existed, the sine rule would give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\sin C}{4}&=\\frac{\\sin45^{\\circ}}{2}\\\\\\sin C&=\\frac{4\\sin45^{\\circ}}{2}\\\\\\sin C&=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\sqrt{2}>1$, this is impossible. Therefore, no such triangle can be constructed.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 32
  },
